-- title:
--   The common-prime setup fits the input-size budget
-- statement:
--   Let $n>0$, $k\in\mathbb N$, and let $R\le3B(n)^2$ be a record count, where $B(n)$ is the executable band count. Write $g(n)$ for band size, $M(n)$ for the global radix, $y(n)$ for the prime window, $m(n)=M(n)(4y(n))^2$, and $d(n)$ for the computed prime-chain depth. Let $s(n)$ and $T_{\rm pool}(n)$ be the size and construction budget of the prime pool, and $P(k,n)$ the defined per-triple arithmetic budget. Then
--
--   $$
--   T_{\rm setup}(m,g,R,M,y,n^k)\le T_{\rm pool}(n)+15(d(n)+1)(3B(n)^2+1)(s(n)+1)^2P(k,n).
--   $$
--
--   The right side is the defined input-size budget. It covers pool generation and all candidate-minimum and weighted band-score calls in the common-prime setup.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/BandPrimeCosts.lean#L115-L129

